Review on the book "The Gambler" by Fyodor Dostoevsky

Dostoevsky had a passion of gambling from 1863-1871.
When his passion of gambling ended, he played at other 
casinos and won a small amount first and then lost far 
more at the. He first brought up his addiction in a letter
he sent to his first wife's sister on 1st September 1866
describing his success from gambling. He also completed
this novel in 1866 under a strict deadline to pay off 
his debts from gambling.

The Gambler is a novel about a young tutor employed to a 
wealthy russian general. The young man lived in a hotel in
Germany, which had a casino in it. The novel reflects the 
author's own experience with his experience with a addiction 
to a casino game named roulette.

The novel's main character is Alexei Ivanovich. A man who fell
in love with a women called Polina, who is the general's stepdaugter.
She was the one who inspired his addiction to gambling by asking him to
gamble for her. His grandmother vists and stays for three days with a 
gambling bug stuck to her. After Alexei drops of his grandmother in the 
station, he returns to the hotel where he finds out des Grieux is taking 
legal actions of selling his stuff morgated to him. The general dies in paris,
and Alexei finds out he's not getting any inheritance. Polina scretely cheats 
on Alexei. The man gives him money in hopes Alexei won't gamble it. Soon Alexei
learns to gamble to survive.


